            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nThis all feels a bit too narrow to be included in the docs. Here is one way to do three scenarios from this issue:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.hipatterns').setup({\r\n  highlighters = {\r\n    -- Highlight whole line including all space to the right\r\n    line_starts = { pattern = '^hello', group = 'DiffAdd', extmark_opts = { line_hl_group = 'DiffAdd' } },\r\n\r\n    -- Highlight whole line but only text\r\n    line_ends = { pattern = '^.*world$', group = 'DiffChange' },\r\n\r\n    -- I don't think there is a way to include a complex \"... or ...\" logic to\r\n    -- `pattern`. See `:h MiniHipatterns.config` for what is possible.\r\n  },\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\n> a definitive guide to using Lua's frontier patterns\r\n\r\nI don't know about a single definitive guide to frontier patterns. Here is [the description from Lua 5.4](https://www.lua.org/manual/5.4/manual.html#6.4.1) reference guide. This works incidentally in Neovim because it uses LuaJIT 5.1, which does have frontier patterns.\r\n\r\n> a snippet that shows how to read all existing styles on a highlight group so they can be incorporated into the call to `vim.api.nvim_set_hl()`\r\n\r\nNot entirely sure what should be done here and why this might be needed. There is a [`get_matches()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/a683bfe8e03293e3bb079e24c94e51977756a3ec/doc/mini-hipatterns.txt#L392) which returns data about matches and should be enough.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
